      "servingTime": "2 hrs 30 mins",
      "yield": "4 servings",
      "tips": "",
      "ingredients": "Ingredients\n4 veal shanks, pat dried \nSalt and pepper, to taste\n1/2 cup all-purpose flour, for dredging\n3 tbsps. olive oil\n2 tbsps. butter\n1 white onion, diced\n1 carrot, diced\n1 celery stalk, diced\n5 cloves garlic, minced\n1 cup Torlasco Barolo DOCG red wine\n2 cups beef or veal broth\n1 can (400g) crushed tomatoes\n2 pcs. bay leaf\n3 sprigs fresh thyme\n2 sprigs fresh rosemary\nZest of 1 lemon\nFor the Gremolata:\nZest of 1 lemon\n5 cloves garlic, minced\n¼ cup fresh parsley, chopped",
      "procedure": "Procedure\nSeason veal shanks with salt and pepper on both sides. Dredge the shanks in flour, shaking off any excess.\nIn a large heavy-bottomed pot or Dutch oven, heat the olive oil and butter over medium-high heat. Add the veal shanks and brown on all sides, about 4-5 minutes per side. Set aside veal shanks. \nIn the same pot, add onions, carrots, and celery. Cook until the vegetables are softened, about 7-8 minutes. Add the minced garlic and cook for another 1-2 minutes until fragrant.\nPour the Torlasco Barolo DOCG Red Wine into the pot, scraping up any browned bits from the bottom. Let the wine simmer for 3-4 minutes until slightly reduced.\nReturn the browned veal shanks to the pot. Add the broth, crushed tomatoes, bay leaves, thyme, rosemary, and lemon zest. Bring the mixture to a simmer.\nReduce the heat to low, cover the pot, and let it simmer gently for 1½ to 2 hours, or until the veal is tender and almost falling off the bone. Occasionally check the liquid level and add more broth if needed.\nWhile the ossobuco is simmering, prepare the gremolata by mixing the lemon zest, minced garlic, and chopped parsley in a small bowl.\nOnce the veal shanks are tender, remove them from the pot and place them on a serving platter. Discard the bay leaf, thyme, and rosemary from the sauce.\nLet the sauce simmer uncovered for a few minutes to thicken. Adjust the seasoning with salt and pepper if needed.\nSpoon the sauce over the veal shanks and sprinkle the gremolata on top. \nServe the Ossobuco alla Milanese with a side of Risotto alla Milanese, accompanied by a glass of Torlasco Barolo DOCG Red Wine."
